cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use dsd-upsample in your work, please cite it as below."
title: "dsd-upsample"
type: software
authors:
  - name: "The dsd-upsample-rs authors"
repository-code: "https://github.com/dsd-toolbox/dsd-upsample-rs"
url: "https://github.com/dsd-toolbox/dsd-upsample-rs"
abstract: "Converts DSF, DFF, WAV and FLAC files to DSD128 or DSD256, and PCM to DSD64, with a one-bit sigma-delta modulator of order 5 to 8, as a Rust library, a C library and a command-line tool. Within a release the output is designed to be byte-identical on every platform (checked on Linux x86_64, macOS arm64 and Windows x64); noise, distortion and overload measurements are published with the commands that reproduce them."
# The code is MIT OR Apache-2.0 (README.md, "Licence and credits"); the documentation and
# measurement data are CC BY 4.0. Only one licence is given here: Zenodo, which archives each
# GitHub release from this file (from 0.1.1 on; 0.1.0 was released before the integration was
# on), reads `license` as a single SPDX string and fails on a list, and "MIT OR Apache-2.0" is
# not a valid value.
# The DOI comes after the v0.1.1 tag, once Zenodo shows the record: the concept DOI (one for
# all versions) as `doi:` after `version`, also named in the GitHub release notes.
license: Apache-2.0
version: "0.1.1"
# The release commit sets the date; it must be the tag's UTC day.
date-released: "2026-11-26"
keywords:
  - "DSD"
  - "DSF"
  - "DSDIFF"
  - "SACD"
  - "sigma-delta modulation"
  - "upsampling"
  - "audio"
  - "Rust"
